<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Rodney</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Walter Anthony Rodney</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walter Rodney was a Guyanese historian, political activist and academic, born into a working-class family in Georgetown. After a PhD in African History in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1966, he taught at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> in Tanzania; his ban from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in 1968 set off protests known as the Rodney riots. His How <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> Underdeveloped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> (1972) argued that exploitation by European imperialists led directly to the modern underdevelopment of most of the continent. A Pan-Africanist and Marxist, he joined the Working People's Alliance in Guyana and was killed by an explosive device in his car in 1980; a Commission of Inquiry concluded it had been a state-ordered killing.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…further: Frantz Fanon in The Wretched of the Earth (1961), the Senegalese scholar Cheikh Anta Diop, who argued in Nations nègres et culture (1954) that ancient Egypt had been a Black African civilisation, a much-debated thesis, and the Guyanese historian <b>Walter Rodney</b> in How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (1972).</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>…and Un-British Rule in India (1901) that British rule drained India’s wealth through charges and profits sent home, and <b>Walter Rodney</b>’s How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (1972) held that colonialism carried the surplus of African labour to Europe while taking from Africans the power to set their own goals.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1965 Kwame Nkrumah called neocolonialism “the last stage of imperialism”, and <b>Walter Rodney</b>, in How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (1972), described a world divided into exploiters and exploited.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Mercantilist practices appeared in early modern Venice, Genoa and Pisa in the control of the Mediterranean bullion trade, and the Guyanese historian <b>Walter Rodney</b> dated the period from the 15th-century Portuguese and Spanish voyages.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
